{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1530459-v5-warrior-setite-loresheet-and-a-bonus","id":1530459,"title":"(V5) Warrior Setite loresheet...and a bonus!","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-02-10T10:20:45-08:00","id":1530459,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon6.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> (V5) Warrior Setite loresheet...and a bonus!","content":"I'm on my shit again, here we go.<br /> <br /> <b>Warriors of Glycon (bloodline loresheet, Ministry only)</b><br /> <br /> We know the deal. They're warrior Setites, blah blah blah. Crunch now, fluff later.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Loyal Unto Death: </b>You are in service to a Minister in a position of leadership, whether in the clan, city, or sect. You gain a Mawla rating of two and share Herd with your Mawla, but you are expected to protect your Mawla, Herd, and cult at all costs, including with your life if need be. Failing to protect them costs you all benefit from this merit; betraying either immediately converts your Mawla into a four-dot Adversary, and you gain the Shunned flaw among Ministers.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022 <b>Blessed are the Generous</b><b>: </b>Once per story, you may call upon a fellow Minister for aid, and access one of their Backgrounds as if it were your own. If you are working to achieve a shared goal the other Minister will not expect repayment; if not, they will expect a boon one stage less than its nominal cost (for instance, an act equivalent to a major boon will cost a minor boon instead). You may not use another Minister's resources to act against them, directly or indirectly.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>Typhonic Maw: </b>If you do not already have the Protean power of Feral Weapons, take it at no cost -- you do not necessarily have the discipline power, but rather a special variant of your own. This power works like Feral Weapons but only allows you to enhance your fangs, which deal three Aggravated rather than the usual two. If you do have (or later acquire) Feral Weapons, when you use the power you may enhance both your claws and fangs at the same time (your fangs still deal 3 Aggravated when using Feral Weapons).<br /> \u200b<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>When You Need Me: </b>When entering torpor voluntarily, your body desiccates and stiffens, becoming identical to that of an Egyptian mummy. While you are in this form, you make no further Rouse checks, and are immune to sunlight and physical damage short of dismemberment that would cause Final Death. However, you can only be awoken from torpor by being fed a single Hunger's worth of blood from a vampire of higher Blood Potency.<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>Typhonic Avatar: </b>If you already have (or later gain) the Protean powers of Shapechange or Metamorphosis, when activating the power you may instead enter a hybrid form between human and your chosen animal. When in this form, replace your own Physical dice pools and Health levels with the animal's, you may use the animal's Exceptional dice pools if higher than your own, and you gain the animal's Special characteristics (if any). You may still speak, and use disciplines which rely on eye contact or speech, in this form. However, this form is extremely hazardous to the Masquerade as it is obviously and inarguably supernatural, and certain to out you as a Minister to anyone with sufficient knowledge of the occult or experience.<br /> <br /> Thoughts?<br /> <br /> And as a bonus, I'm working on a &quot;Descendant of Vykos&quot; loresheet, but coming up a little short on ideas for it. So far, I have:<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022 <b>Peter Yeeter: </b>If you do not already have it, gain the Throwing specialty for Athletics. In addition, when throwing improvised weapons which deal superficial Health damage, you may opt to deal superficial Willpower damage instead.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-10T10:41:09-08:00","id":1530460,"title":"","content":"As a Setite and Warriors of Glycon fan, I greatly appreciate the attention to the bloodline.<br /> I don't play V5 and can't comment on the rules you proposed because I don't understand them much system-wise.<br /> <br /> Fluff wise, I must correct something: unless the Warriors of Glycon are mentioned in V5 manuals and I'm unaware of it, there is a confusion there between them and Warrior Setites:<br /> <br /> Warriors of Glycon are the rulers of the clan in the Dark Ages and go extinct at some point between 1244 (when the Witches of Echidna are presumed extinct) and the Modern Nights due to the machinations of the Setite Priests. They are a full bloodline with their own clan weakness (different from that of the Priests, i.e. double damage from sunlight) and Potence, Obfuscate and Serpentis as in-clan disciplines. The infant of a Warrior of Glycon is a Warrior of Glycon, with the same discipline spread regardless of training.<br /> <br /> Warrior Setites are the trained guard of the Priests. They exist at least since the Dark Ages since they are mentioned (or hinted at) in the description of the Warriors of Glycon as part of the threats to their position and existence. They are Priests by blood, but due to training as ghouls before the Embrace, they have Potence instead of Obfuscate as in-clan disciplines. Thus their weakness is the same as that of the Priests; their disciplines are Potence, Presence and Serpentis; and their infants are normal Priests with Obfuscate, Presence and Serpentis <i>unless</i> they receive the same ghoul training before the Embrace. They serve the Priests, unlike the Warriors of Glycon, who rule over the Priests.<br /> <br /> In short: I like the idea of the lore sheet, but the name should be changed into &quot;Warrior Setites&quot; or their nickname, &quot;Horned Vipers&quot;, to avoid confusion with the extinct VDA20 bloodline.<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-10T11:20:43-08:00","id":1530462,"title":"","content":"The main difference between regular Warrior Setites and the Glycon variety seems to be motivation, the Glycon variety are independents rulers who exalt strength and violence while the normal Warriors are proud to be mere servants as they cover for the weaknesses of some patron like their sire or a Temple official. The Glycon like obfuscate as the possibility of not even seeing punishment coming would make their subordinates too scared to risk revolting, while the Warriors want you to see them coming and know you can't do anything to stop them<br /> <br /> The first and second dots for this loresheet make it feel more appropriate for the regular Warrior Setites and its a pretty useful one for them so kudos. The fourth dot too makes them seem reliant on trusting someone older or more powerful than them to be awakened which might be too risky/humiliating for a Glycon. I'm also in favor of removing mentions of Glycon from the loresheet's name<br /> <br /> I could definitely see the Glycon bloodline reemerge though now that the Ministry is more secular, the orthodox Setites have lost a lot of influence over the whole clan so it'd be harder to stamp out those who serve themselves rather than the will of Set. You should do a loresheet involving rogue Ministers who rule using threats of violence and combat prowess, thats def in an Anarch's wheelhouse","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28284-abugintherug","name":"abugintherug","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar28284_2.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-11T09:10:25-08:00","id":1530554,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the input.<br /> <br /> First and foremost, mechanically there aren't really <i>bloodline</i> bloodlines in v5. The only real mechanical support for older-edition bloodlines is among the Hecata, where as an optional rule Hecata can swap out one innate discipline for another in accordance to their loresheet (Nagaraja swap Fortitude for Dominate, Samedi swap Auspex for Obfuscate, all the Giovanni families swap Auspex for Dominate), or take ECD for those disciplines at four merit points. They can also optionally take <i>certain</i> flaws totalling two dots as variant banes, or those flaws are recommended at character generation for players who want an experience more authentic to older editions (starving decay, ugly/repulsive, stench, and organovore are the big ones).<br /> <br /> Additionally, certain Hecata bloodlines have merits that flirt with ECD but don't go all the way, spot unique uses for certain discipline powers, or expand the uses of disciplines beyond the typical. It's not unique to the Hecata -- the Banu Haqim have a loresheet or two that replicates viziers and their powers, without introducing the bloodline altogether -- they just happen to be the most prominent clan with loresheets which do it.<br /> <br /> But, it's also made clear straight up Hecata are not to be considered a traditional clan, but more akin to a sect all to its own.<br /> <br /> So, it's in that context I worked up the loresheet as such. Three of the powers approximate OG Serpentis, within the constraint of v5 mechanics and the general balance and flavor of loresheets. Take for example Typhonic Maw as I wrote it. In v5 vampires deal a set amount of aggravated (two) when biting, and either just chomp (taking called shot penalties) and auto-grapple if they succeed, or grapple and bite (requiring a grapple check first). Feeding is considered the default and automatic upon players' choice, when biting. Feral weapons in v5 can augment bite, and if bite is augmented the vampire negates called shot penalties for biting.<br /> <br /> So, the &quot;big sleeper&quot; benefits of Tongue of the Asp and/or Typhonic Maw are already baked into v5 mechanics. The best way to approximate it is to give a &quot;phantom&quot; feral weapons power in the same vein as how the Nagaraja loresheet augments Obfuscate, or how the al-Ashrad loresheet augments Auspex. From there, there were two ways to do it: buff bite damage (which has precedent) or provide a grapple check bonus when biting (which doesn't, at least not within the remit of protean or loresheets): as I wrote it I opted for the former. But now that I've thought of it, I might rewrite it to be the latter instead since it makes a lot more sense.<br /> <br /> As far as the lore, I'm aware of it. Like with the other loresheets I've homebrewed, I'm taking a &quot;Dark Ages revivalist&quot; approach. What I mean by that, is between the loss of elders to the Beckoning and Second Inquisition, a lot of suppressed knowledge has been set free and a lot of &quot;common&quot; knowledge has been lost. So, one ends up with a lot of younger vampires learning things they were never intended to learn (or that elders never considered relevant), but losing vital context and major secrets in the process. Things get confused, and what comes out the other side is something of a mishmash/hybridization of new and old that best-fits post-Beckoning, post-Second Inquisition, circumstances.<br /> <br /> For instance, when I did the &quot;Ventrue <i>antitribu</i>&quot; loresheet I contextualized it as younger, disenfranchised, Anarch Ventrue cross-pollenating with Sabbat defectors. What came out the other side wasn't far off from the <i>antitribu</i> themselves -- a militant, historically revisionist, Anarch movement that romanticizes crusader Ventrue while vilifying those who &quot;betrayed&quot; and &quot;displaced&quot; them -- but absent the cyclical violence mission creep suffered under centuries of Sabbat membership.<br /> <br /> Or, when I did the &quot;Telyav&quot; loresheet, they're not Telyavelic Tremere at all by blood. They're Tremere of Houses Ipsissimus and Carna, who after the Fall of Vienna learned about the Telyavelic Tremere and House Diedne, and their respective fates. In the course of it they rediscovered Sielanic Thaumaturgy, and even managed to enforce it with some <i>old</i> school Hermetic tricks. They just happen to call themselves Shepherds, to honor those lost to Tremere duplicity and betrayal in the name of orthodoxy.<br /> <br /> With this, I envisioned something of the inverse happening. The Setites haven't really changed, so much as rebranded and adapted their practices to the 21st Century, and the cultures in which they find themselves. There's not much talk of Lector-priests in v5 (of which I'm aware) although one would assume they'd be orthodox Setites to a member, leaving the question of what roles warrior Setites would serve in the Ministry, but more importantly how they would brand themselves absent overt reference to Set. No better way to kill multiple birds with one stone, than appropriating a title with pedigree and history, creatively recontextualizing it, and applying it -- pretty much the same as how major religions appropriate from one another for the sake of legitimacy.<br /> <br /> Are they &quot;real&quot; Warriors of Glycon? no, of course not. They're just called that. Fantastic way to delegitimize anyone who might show up speaking inconvenient truths about Setite elders, who Setites really serve, and whether Lector-priests have <i>really</i> changed their ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-13T13:02:45-08:00","id":1530750,"title":"","content":"This is certainly a nice and good enough Setite Loresheet and story background description. It is especially interesting for me that a benefit can under some circumstances undergo conversion into an adversary. Also it is good you included Boons as a field to take actions and gain benefits upon. Also the lenght and size of the descriptions seems right and appropriate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude0e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-13T17:37:27-08:00","id":1530771,"title":"","content":"I love every time someone dives deep into the lore and this is a really good loresheet. Great job!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-18T17:28:15-08:00","id":1531077,"title":"","content":"Thanks. After giving it a think, I'm going to revise Typhonic Maw to this:<br /> <br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>Typhonic Maw: </b>If you do not already have the Protean power of Feral Weapons, take it at no cost -- you do not necessarily have the discipline power, but rather a special variant of your own. This power works like Feral Weapons but only allows you to enhance your fangs, which deal three Aggravated rather than the usual two. If you do have (or later acquire) Feral Weapons, when you use the power to enhance your fangs, add your Protean rating as bonus dice on grapple tests after successfully biting an opponent (your fangs still deal 3 Aggravated when using Feral Weapons).<br /> <br /> That makes it a little cleaner, and provides a nice utility that parallels the Potence power Relentless Grasp, as a nod to prior-editions' warriors having Potence instead of Obfuscate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/431-theodrim","name":"Theodrim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar431_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1841"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}